        {
          n: "1",
          answer: `<p><b>Failure of classical physics.</b> Rayleigh and Jeans treated the cavity radiation with classical equipartition (every mode has average energy k<sub>B</sub>T) and obtained</p><div class="eq">u(ν) dν = (8πk<sub>B</sub>T/c<sup>3</sup>) ν<sup>2</sup> dν</div><p>This fits the measured spectrum at low frequencies but rises as ν<sup>2</sup> without limit, so the total energy ∫u dν would be infinite. Experiment shows the curve reaching a peak and falling to zero at high frequency. This failure in the ultraviolet is the <b>ultraviolet catastrophe</b>. Wien’s empirical law u = Aν<sup>3</sup>e<sup>−Bν/T</sup> fitted the high-frequency side but underestimated the infrared, so neither classical attempt worked everywhere.</p><figure data-fig="m1-uv-catastrophe"></figure><p><b>Planck’s hypothesis (1900).</b></p><ul><li>Energy is exchanged between the wall oscillators and the radiation only in discrete quanta.</li><li>Each quantum has energy E = hν, so an oscillator can have only 0, hν, 2hν, 3hν, ….</li><li>The occupation of these levels follows Boltzmann statistics, probability ∝ e<sup>−E/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup>; the average energy per oscillator is then hν/(e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> − 1) instead of k<sub>B</sub>T.</li><li>High-frequency oscillators need a large quantum hν ≫ k<sub>B</sub>T, which is rarely available, so they carry almost no energy. This removes the catastrophe.</li></ul><p>Planck’s radiation law:</p><div class="eq">u(ν) dν = (8πhν<sup>3</sup>/c<sup>3</sup>) · 1/(e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> − 1) dν</div><p><b>Low frequency, hν ≪ k<sub>B</sub>T.</b> Using e<sup>x</sup> ≈ 1 + x, e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> − 1 ≈ hν/k<sub>B</sub>T, so u(ν) dν ≈ (8πhν<sup>3</sup>/c<sup>3</sup>)(k<sub>B</sub>T/hν) dν = (8πν<sup>2</sup>k<sub>B</sub>T/c<sup>3</sup>) dν: the Rayleigh–Jeans law.</p><p><b>High frequency, hν ≫ k<sub>B</sub>T.</b> e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> − 1 ≈ e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup>, so u(ν) dν ≈ (8πhν<sup>3</sup>/c<sup>3</sup>) e<sup>−hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> dν: Wien’s law with A = 8πh/c<sup>3</sup> and B = h/k<sub>B</sub>, which falls to zero as ν → ∞.</p><p>So Planck’s single formula agrees with experiment at all frequencies and contains both earlier laws as limits.</p>`
        },
        {
          n: "2",
          answer: `<p><b>Classical particles (macroscopic balls).</b> Balls fired at a wall with two openings: with only slit 1 open the detector records a hump P<sub>1</sub>, with only slit 2 a hump P<sub>2</sub>. With both open the counts simply add: P<sub>12</sub> = P<sub>1</sub> + P<sub>2</sub>, a broad two-humped or single smooth distribution with no interference.</p><p><b>Quantum particles (electrons).</b> With both slits open each electron arrives at the screen as a single dot, but after many electrons the dots build up alternating bright and dark fringes, even when electrons are sent one at a time (the Hitachi experiment showed the build-up from a few to 160 000 electrons). At some points fewer electrons arrive with both slits open than with one, which is impossible for classical particles.</p><figure data-fig="m1-double-slit-particles"></figure><p><b>Explanation.</b> Each electron is described by a wavefunction; the amplitudes from the two slits add before squaring:</p><div class="eq">Ψ<sub>12</sub> = Ψ<sub>1</sub> + Ψ<sub>2</sub> ⇒ P<sub>12</sub> = |Ψ<sub>1</sub>|<sup>2</sup> + |Ψ<sub>2</sub>|<sup>2</sup> + Ψ<sub>1</sub>*Ψ<sub>2</sub> + Ψ<sub>2</sub>*Ψ<sub>1</sub></div><p>The last two terms are interference terms, so P<sub>12</sub> ≠ P<sub>1</sub> + P<sub>2</sub>. The fringe spacing matches the de Broglie wavelength λ = h/p, which shows that electrons behave as waves. For a macroscopic ball λ = h/mv is far smaller than any slit, so the fringes are unobservably close and the classical result P<sub>1</sub> + P<sub>2</sub> is recovered.</p>`
        },
        {
          n: "3a",
          answer: `<p>Compton shift Δλ = (h/m<sub>e</sub>c)(1 − cos θ), with the constants given in the paper.</p><div class="worked"><div class="solve"><div class="eq">h/m<sub>e</sub>c = 6.626 × 10<sup>−34</sup> / (9.109 × 10<sup>−31</sup> × 3 × 10<sup>8</sup>) = 2.425 × 10<sup>−12</sup> m</div><div class="eq">(i) Δλ = 2.425 pm × (1 − cos 60°) = 2.425 × 0.5 = 1.212 pm</div><div class="eq">λ′ = 142 + 1.212 = 143.2 pm</div><div class="eq">(ii) Maximum shift at θ = 180°: Δλ<sub>max</sub> = 2h/m<sub>e</sub>c = 4.849 pm</div></div><div class="result"><p>λ′ ≈ 143.2 pm; maximum shift ≈ 4.85 pm (for back-scattering, θ = 180°)</p></div></div>`
        },
        {
          n: "3b",
          answer: `<p>Both gain the same kinetic energy eV (same charge, same voltage), so p = √(2meV) and λ = h/√(2meV).</p><div class="worked"><div class="solve"><div class="eq">Electron: λ<sub>e</sub> = 6.626 × 10<sup>−34</sup> / √(2 × 9.109 × 10<sup>−31</sup> × 1.6 × 10<sup>−19</sup> × 5000) = 1.736 × 10<sup>−11</sup> m</div><div class="eq">Proton: λ<sub>p</sub> = 6.626 × 10<sup>−34</sup> / √(2 × 1.673 × 10<sup>−27</sup> × 1.6 × 10<sup>−19</sup> × 5000) = 4.050 × 10<sup>−13</sup> m</div><div class="eq">λ<sub>e</sub>/λ<sub>p</sub> = √(m<sub>p</sub>/m<sub>e</sub>) = 42.86</div></div><div class="result"><p>λ<sub>e</sub> ≈ 1.74 × 10<sup>−11</sup> m, λ<sub>p</sub> ≈ 4.05 × 10<sup>−13</sup> m</p></div></div><p>Equal charge means equal energy, but the proton is about 1837 times heavier, so its momentum is √1837 ≈ 43 times larger and its wavelength about 43 times shorter. The lighter electron shows wave effects much more readily.</p>`
        },
        {
          n: "4a",
          answer: `<p>Heisenberg: Δx Δp ≥ ℏ/2 with Δp = mΔv, so Δv<sub>min</sub> = ℏ/(2mΔx).</p><div class="worked"><div class="solve"><div class="eq">ℏ = h/2π = 1.055 × 10<sup>−34</sup> J s</div><div class="eq">Δv = 1.055 × 10<sup>−34</sup> / (2 × 70 × 1.0) = 7.53 × 10<sup>−37</sup> m/s</div></div><div class="result"><p>Δv<sub>min</sub> ≈ 7.5 × 10<sup>−37</sup> m/s</p></div></div><p>This is immeasurably small, so the uncertainty principle has no noticeable effect on everyday objects. Quantum effects matter only for very small masses such as electrons, where m is about 10<sup>−30</sup> kg and Δv becomes large.</p>`
        },
        {
          n: "4b",
          answer: `<p>Integrate Planck’s energy density over all frequencies:</p><div class="worked"><div class="solve"><div class="eq">U = ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup> (8πhν<sup>3</sup>/c<sup>3</sup>) dν / (e<sup>hν/k<sub>B</sub>T</sup> − 1)</div><div class="eq">Put x = hν/k<sub>B</sub>T, ν = k<sub>B</sub>Tx/h, dν = (k<sub>B</sub>T/h) dx</div><div class="eq">U = (8πk<sub>B</sub><sup>4</sup>T<sup>4</sup>/h<sup>3</sup>c<sup>3</sup>) ∫<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup> x<sup>3</sup> dx/(e<sup>x</sup> − 1)</div><div class="eq">∫<sub>0</sub><sup>∞</sup> x<sup>3</sup>/(e<sup>x</sup> − 1) dx = π<sup>4</sup>/15 = 6.494</div><div class="eq">U = (8π<sup>5</sup>k<sub>B</sub><sup>4</sup>/15h<sup>3</sup>c<sup>3</sup>) T<sup>4</sup> = aT<sup>4</sup>, a = 7.56 × 10<sup>−16</sup> J m<sup>−3</sup> K<sup>−4</sup></div></div><div class="result"><p>U ∝ T<sup>4</sup></p></div></div><p>The power radiated per unit area is (c/4)U = σT<sup>4</sup> with σ = 2π<sup>5</sup>k<sub>B</sub><sup>4</sup>/15h<sup>3</sup>c<sup>2</sup> = 5.67 × 10<sup>−8</sup> W m<sup>−2</sup> K<sup>−4</sup>: the Stefan–Boltzmann law follows from Planck’s law. Doubling T multiplies the radiated energy by 16.</p>`
        },
        {
          n: "5a",
          answer: `<p><b>Matter waves.</b> de Broglie (1924) proposed that every moving material particle has a wave associated with it, of wavelength λ = h/p = h/mv (the de Broglie wavelength). These waves are called matter waves.</p><p><b>Properties.</b></p><ul><li>λ = h/mv: the lighter or slower the particle, the longer its wavelength; a stationary particle (v = 0) has no defined wavelength and a very heavy body has an unobservably small one.</li><li>For a charged particle accelerated through V: λ = h/√(2mqV); for an electron λ ≈ 12.27/√V Å.</li><li>Matter waves are not electromagnetic; they are produced by any moving particle, charged or neutral.</li><li>The quantity that varies is the wavefunction Ψ; |Ψ|<sup>2</sup> gives the probability of finding the particle, so matter waves are probability waves.</li><li>Their phase velocity can exceed c, but the group velocity equals the particle velocity, so no physical signal travels faster than light.</li><li>Electron diffraction by crystals (Davisson–Germer, G. P. Thomson) confirmed λ = h/p.</li></ul>`
        },
        {
          n: "5b",
          answer: `<p><b>Set-up.</b> Silver is heated in an oven to give a beam of neutral silver atoms; the beam is collimated by narrow slits, passes between the poles of a magnet shaped to give a <b>non-uniform</b> field B<sub>z</sub>(z), and strikes a detector plate. Silver has one unpaired outer electron, so the atom’s magnetic moment is that of one electron spin.</p><figure data-fig="m1-stern-gerlach"></figure><p><b>Principle.</b> A magnetic moment μ in a field has energy E = −μ·B. A uniform field only twists the moment; a field gradient exerts a force F<sub>z</sub> = μ<sub>z</sub> ∂B<sub>z</sub>/∂z, so atoms are deflected up or down according to μ<sub>z</sub>. For an electron μ = −g(e/2m)S with g ≈ 2.</p><p><b>Classical expectation.</b> Moments oriented at random would give all values of μ<sub>z</sub>, so a continuous smear on the plate.</p><p><b>Observation.</b> The beam splits into exactly two spots, one above and one below the centre line.</p><p><b>Conclusions.</b></p><ul><li>The electron has an intrinsic angular momentum, spin, not caused by any rotation of a charged ball.</li><li>The spin component along the field is quantised with only two values, S<sub>z</sub> = +ℏ/2 or −ℏ/2 (spin up and spin down).</li><li>Measuring along another axis (x) again gives only ±ℏ/2.</li><li>It is a real two-state quantum system, the basis of qubits, and it shows that measurement gives only certain allowed values (quantisation).</li></ul>`
        }
